---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const pay = assertion('the-biggest-employer-is-not-the-biggest-payer')
const commute = assertion('half-the-jobs-here-are-held-by-outsiders')

const paySpec = bar(
  pay.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  pay.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Average annual pay' },
)

const commuteSpec = bar(
  commute.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  commute.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'People', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Base
  title="Work"
  lede="What work there is in Allen County, what it pays, and how many of the people doing it live somewhere else."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Thirteen phases of counting jobs, and none of counting pay</h2>
    <p>
      This corpus has held the county's employment since its ninth phase — by sector, back to 1969,
      through the manufacturing decline and out the other side. It had never held a wage. The
      Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages publishes both, and the two rank the county
      differently.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={pay} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="pay"
      title="Average annual pay by sector, 2024"
      caption="Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages, annual averages, all ownerships. Pay is total annual wages divided by average annual employment — the file publishes both terms and not this ratio."
      columns={['Sector', 'Average annual pay']}
      rows={pay.figures.map((f) => [f.label, '$' + f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="pay" set:html={JSON.stringify(paySpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Across all 49,690 covered jobs the average weekly wage is $1,131 — 89.3 per cent of Ohio's
      and 77.8 per cent of the country's. A worker here earns about four fifths of what a worker in
      the United States does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Manufacturing is 17.9 per cent of the jobs and 28.0 per cent of the wages, the only sector in
      the county whose share of pay is more than half again its share of employment. The period this
      site calls deindustrialization ended with manufacturing still the best-paid large sector here;
      what replaced the jobs did not replace the pay. The corpus holds one year of wages and cannot
      say when that gap opened.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Half the jobs here belong to somebody else's county</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={commute} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="commute"
      title="The journey to work, 2022"
      caption="LEHD Origin-Destination Employment Statistics, all jobs. In-commuters come mostly from the ring of rural counties — Auglaize, Putnam, Van Wert, Hardin, Hancock."
      columns={['', 'People']}
      rows={commute.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="commute" set:html={JSON.stringify(commuteSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Lima holds 46.1 per cent of the county's jobs and 37.2 per cent of its employed residents, so
      the city is a net importer of workers inside a county that is a net importer of workers. And
      the three flows are alike in what they pay: 47.1, 50.8 and 49.4 per cent of them are in the
      file's top earnings band. The obvious story — that the good jobs go to people who drive in —
      is not in the data.
    </p>
    <p>
      One figure on this page is reported and not read. Franklin County — Columbus, a hundred miles
      away — is the second-largest recorded destination for Allen County residents who work outside
      it, above two counties that share a border. The 1,723 are spread thinly across many Columbus
      tracts. A block-to-block table of where people work cannot be published as collected, and this
      is what a disclosure-protected file looks like at small grain: the sums are sound and the
      cells are not. The county totals are trusted here because an unrelated programme counted
      49,690 jobs where this one counted 48,730.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
